import type { Lesson } from "./lessons";

export const lesson014 = {
  number: 14,
  module: "Module 1: How a Chip Is Physically Made",
  title: "CMP Consumables — Slurry, Pads and Why a Capital Tool Creates Recurring Revenue",
  date: "2026-08-20",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 009: Yield turns process quality into economics because more good dies from the same wafer lowers effective chip cost.

Lesson 010: Ion implantation places dopants into silicon at controlled dose, depth and angle to create electrically useful regions.

Lesson 011: Annealing repairs implantation damage and activates dopants while the fab manages the wafer's thermal budget.

Lesson 012: Cleaning repeatedly resets the wafer surface so particles, residues and drying damage do not poison later steps.

Lesson 013: CMP combines chemistry and mechanical polishing to flatten the wafer repeatedly so later layers can be built accurately.

The big idea

Yesterday we learned the CMP tool. Today focus on what the tool consumes every time it runs.

A CMP system needs two especially important consumables: slurry and a polishing pad.

The simple mental model is:

CMP tool = the machine.

Slurry = the engineered liquid that chemically prepares and abrasively helps remove the target material.

Pad = the engineered surface that transfers mechanical force and carries slurry across the wafer.

That distinction matters economically. A fab may buy a CMP tool once and use it for years, but it must keep buying slurry, pads and related consumables while wafers are being processed.

This creates a useful semiconductor-business pattern: installed equipment can generate recurring materials demand tied more closely to wafer production than to new-fab capex.

1. What is CMP slurry?

Slurry is not just liquid sandpaper.

It is an engineered mixture whose job is to remove one material at a controlled rate while minimizing damage to everything that should remain.

A slurry commonly contains:

- abrasive particles, often silica, ceria or another engineered material;
- water or another carrier liquid;
- oxidizers or other chemicals that react with the target surface;
- additives that control pH, corrosion, particle behavior and selectivity.

The chemistry depends on what the fab is polishing. Copper, tungsten, oxide and emerging metals do not want the same recipe.

Entegris, for example, sells slurries for tungsten, dielectric materials, copper, barrier materials, aluminum and emerging materials such as molybdenum.

So the correct mental model is not "one CMP slurry market." It is a portfolio of highly specific polishing problems.

2. Why chemistry and abrasives work together

Imagine trying to remove a metal line with pure mechanical force.

You could polish harder, but that can increase scratches and damage.

Instead, the slurry chemistry can change the topmost surface into a form that is easier to remove. The abrasive particles and pad then help carry that reacted material away.

The fab tunes this interaction to achieve a target removal rate.

Too slow means poor throughput.

Too fast can make endpoint control harder and increase over-polish risk.

The winning slurry is therefore not necessarily the one that removes material fastest. It is the one that produces the best total process result: removal rate, selectivity, uniformity, low defects and acceptable cost.

3. Selectivity is the key concept

Suppose copper fills a trench surrounded by dielectric material.

The fab wants to remove excess copper above the trench without excessively attacking the surrounding material.

That ratio is a form of selectivity.

A slurry can be engineered so its chemistry interacts much more strongly with one material than another.

This is similar to what you learned in etch: semiconductor manufacturing repeatedly depends on removing material A while protecting material B.

As chips introduce new metals and more complicated stacks, slurry development can become harder because the supplier must optimize several interacting materials at once.

4. Defects can erase the benefit of faster polishing

Slurry contains particles on purpose, but uncontrolled particles are dangerous.

Agglomerates, contaminants or poorly controlled abrasive distributions can scratch the wafer or create defects.

That is why slurry manufacturing, filtration, storage and delivery matter.

Entegris is strategically interesting here because it sells not only CMP slurry and pads but also filters, high-purity packaging, fluid monitoring and post-CMP cleaning products.

This gives us a broader systems lesson:

The slurry formulation can be excellent and still fail economically if contamination control around the slurry is poor.

Semiconductor process performance often depends on the whole delivery system, not just the headline material.

5. What does the polishing pad actually do?

The pad is the surface pressed against the wafer during CMP.

It must distribute pressure, transport slurry, remove reacted material and maintain stable contact across a 300 mm wafer.

Pads are commonly engineered from polyurethane or related materials.

Important pad properties include:

- hardness;
- compressibility;
- pore structure;
- surface texture;
- groove pattern;
- chemical compatibility;
- wear rate.

Entegris describes its NexPlanar pads as thermoset polyurethane products offered with different hardness and porosity options for different CMP applications.

A harder pad may help with planarity but can create different defect tradeoffs than a softer pad. There is no universal best pad.

The pad, slurry and process recipe are a system.

6. Why pads wear out

The pad is repeatedly exposed to pressure, motion, chemistry and abrasive material.

Its surface gradually changes.

If the pad becomes glazed or its texture changes too much, slurry distribution and removal behavior can deteriorate.

So fabs condition the pad to restore the surface and eventually replace it.

This creates recurring demand.

The same fab can run the same CMP tool for years while repeatedly purchasing pads, slurry, conditioners, brushes, filters and cleaning chemistry.

That is economically different from selling another multi-million-dollar capital tool.

7. Capital spending versus wafer-volume spending

This distinction is worth memorizing.

Equipment demand is driven heavily by capacity additions, technology transitions and fab capital budgets.

Consumables demand is driven more directly by how much qualified manufacturing actually runs and how much material each wafer consumes.

That does not make consumables non-cyclical. If wafer starts fall sharply, slurry and pad usage can fall. Customers can also reduce inventories or negotiate pricing.

But the revenue profile can be steadier than new-equipment sales because fabs need consumables to keep already-installed tools producing wafers.

This is one reason investors often value high-share semiconductor consumables businesses differently from pure equipment businesses.

8. A simple unit-economics model

Suppose a fab processes 100,000 wafers per month.

Assume, only for illustration, that each wafer averages 15 CMP passes.

That means 1.5 million CMP wafer-passes per month.

Now suppose the slurry, pad wear and directly related consumables average an illustrative $4 of cost per pass.

That would imply about $6 million of monthly CMP-consumables demand, or roughly $72 million annualized.

Those are not estimates for a real fab. Actual costs vary dramatically by recipe, material, supplier contract and process.

The point is the equation:

Wafer starts × CMP passes per wafer × consumables cost per pass = rough recurring CMP consumables pool.

Compare that with yesterday's equipment equation:

Wafer starts × CMP passes ÷ effective tool throughput = rough CMP tool capacity required.

One physical process therefore creates two different revenue models.

9. Macro scale: how large is the consumables market?

Exact market estimates vary by research methodology, which is itself a useful warning against false precision.

A 2025 Global Info Research estimate put the combined global CMP slurry-and-pads market at about $3.0 billion in 2024, with slurry representing roughly two-thirds of that market and 300 mm wafers roughly three-quarters of demand.

A separate July 2026 Fortune Business Insights estimate put slurry alone at about $2.6 billion in 2025 and projected roughly 7.7% annual growth through 2034.

Do not memorize both forecasts as truth. Memorize the scale:

CMP slurry and pads are a several-billion-dollar annual consumables market, not a $50 billion equipment category.

That denominator matters. Even strong growth in a niche can be economically meaningful for a focused supplier while remaining small relative to total semiconductor manufacturing spend.

SEMI's February 2026 CMP market report also says advanced logic, 3D NAND and multi-gate structures are supporting renewed demand for slurries and pads, while new materials such as cobalt and molybdenum are changing planarization requirements.

10. Who are the important suppliers?

For slurry and pads, remember a broader supplier set than the CMP equipment market.

Entegris became much larger in CMP materials through its acquisition of CMC Materials. Its portfolio now spans slurry, pads, post-CMP cleaning chemistry, brushes, filtration and related contamination-control products.

DuPont is another major name, especially in polishing pads and CMP materials.

Fujimi is important in precision polishing and slurry.

Resonac and several Asian specialty-chemical suppliers also participate.

Third-party market estimates differ on exact share, so do not anchor too hard on a single percentage. The more durable point is that this market is concentrated enough for qualification and know-how to matter, but it is not a one-company monopoly.

11. Why qualification creates stickiness

Imagine a fab has spent months tuning a copper CMP process around one slurry and pad combination.

Engineers know its removal rate, defect signature, endpoint behavior, temperature sensitivity and interaction with the tool.

A cheaper competing slurry appears.

The fab cannot simply pour it into the line tomorrow.

It must test whether the new material changes yield, uniformity, corrosion, scratches, dishing, erosion or downstream process behavior.

The potential savings on slurry are tiny compared with the value of wafers that could be damaged by a bad transition.

That creates qualification-based switching costs.

This is one of the most important recurring moats in semiconductor materials: the product itself may not look impossible to reproduce, but replacing a qualified material inside a high-yield process can be economically dangerous.

12. But recurring revenue does not mean automatic pricing power

This is where investment analysis often becomes too simplistic.

A consumable can be recurring and still be a mediocre business.

Ask four questions:

How differentiated is the formulation?

How costly is requalification?

How many credible alternate suppliers exist?

How large is the consumable cost relative to the yield risk it controls?

If the material is easy to substitute, customers can pressure price.

If the material is deeply process-specific and a tiny portion of wafer cost but materially affects yield, the supplier can have much stronger economics.

The best semiconductor consumables are often inexpensive relative to the value they protect.

13. Why new materials can create opportunity

Semiconductor roadmaps periodically introduce new materials because old materials stop scaling well enough.

That creates a new polishing problem.

The old slurry may have the wrong chemistry or selectivity. Suppliers must formulate and qualify a new solution.

Entegris specifically highlights emerging interconnect materials such as molybdenum as an opportunity for new slurry development.

This can reset competitive positions.

An incumbent benefits from customer relationships and process knowledge, but a technology transition can also give a challenger an opening if it solves the new material better.

So materials transitions are moments to watch closely.

14. Logic, DRAM and NAND do not consume CMP identically

Leading-edge logic uses CMP across transistor formation and multilayer interconnects.

DRAM requires highly uniform planarization across dense memory structures and interconnects.

3D NAND builds extremely tall vertical stacks, which can create thick films and difficult topography.

That means wafer growth alone does not determine consumables growth.

You also need to know CMP intensity per wafer and whether newer structures use more expensive or more specialized recipes.

This is the same expert habit we are building across the course:

Units × process intensity × price/content per unit = supplier revenue opportunity.

15. AI linkage: real, but indirect

AI demand raises demand for advanced logic and HBM, and both require sophisticated wafer processing.

That can increase CMP consumables demand through more wafers, more difficult structures or higher-value recipes.

But saying "AI is growing, therefore CMP slurry is a great investment" skips too many steps.

The stronger chain is:

AI compute demand → advanced logic/HBM demand → wafer starts and process complexity → CMP passes and recipe difficulty → qualified slurry/pad consumption → supplier volume, mix and pricing.

Every arrow can weaken the thesis.

A supplier may lose share. Process changes may reduce consumption. Customers may dual-source. Growth may occur in a recipe where the supplier is weak.

This is why understanding the physical flow improves investment analysis.

16. The economic comparison to remember

CMP equipment and CMP consumables solve the same manufacturing problem but monetize differently.

Applied Materials and EBARA primarily expose you to the capital equipment layer: tools, installed capacity, upgrades and service.

Entegris, DuPont and other materials suppliers expose you more directly to recurring slurry/pad usage, though each company has many businesses beyond CMP.

Neither model is automatically superior.

Equipment can capture large dollars when fabs build aggressively or process intensity jumps.

Consumables can compound with the installed base and wafer volume while benefiting from qualification stickiness.

A sophisticated investor asks which layer captures the incremental margin dollars from the specific technology transition being studied.

The value-chain map today

Wafer enters a CMP tool

↓

Slurry chemistry reacts with the target material

↓

Abrasive particles + pad mechanically remove the reacted surface

↓

Pad conditioning keeps the polishing surface effective

↓

Slurry filtration and delivery control particles and contamination

↓

Post-CMP cleaning removes residue

↓

Every processed wafer consumes materials, creating recurring demand around the installed tool base

Remember these 7 things

1. Slurry is an engineered chemical-and-abrasive system, not generic polishing liquid.

2. The pad controls mechanical contact, slurry transport and planarity; its hardness, porosity and grooves are part of the process recipe.

3. Slurry and pads are recurring consumables because fabs must replenish them while CMP tools keep running.

4. Think in two equations: tool capacity depends on wafer passes divided by throughput; consumables demand depends on wafer starts × passes × consumable content per pass.

5. The combined slurry-and-pad market is on the order of several billion dollars annually, with slurry the larger portion in common third-party estimates.

6. Entegris, DuPont, Fujimi and other specialty-material suppliers compete through formulation, defect control, qualification and customer integration rather than monopoly equipment positions.

7. Recurring does not automatically mean high-quality economics: always test differentiation, qualification cost, alternate suppliers and the value protected relative to material cost.

Quick check

Why might a fab refuse to switch to a slurry that is 20% cheaper?

Answer: if the existing slurry is deeply qualified and the cheaper alternative creates even a small risk of lower yield, scratches, corrosion or process instability, the potential wafer losses can dwarf the consumable savings.

